egory" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066049/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a6efe70f913ac229aee69f8912e1a9696608b9cb84deb88419fa9c2cdf97072"} {"question" "q-140134" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$\n\n$$\nS={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066049/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a6efe70f913ac229aee69f8912e1a9696608b9cb84deb88419fa9c2cdf97072"} {"question" "q-140134" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066049/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ae6990cd4e9a522228d2d882f29c3f128d059f74d476cbccc3616545ef941417"} {"question" "q-140134" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066049/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a6efe70f913ac229aee69f8912e1a9696608b9cb84deb88419fa9c2